1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,558
Everywhere we look on Earth, where
there is liquid water, there is life.

2
00:00:10,638 --> 00:00:13,810
From the deepest trenches
to the driest deserts,

3
00:00:13,890 --> 00:00:18,326
water seems to be the one
ingredient life cannot do without.

4
00:00:18,406 --> 00:00:23,501
So when astronomers search for life
beyond our planet, they follow the water.

5
00:00:23,581 --> 00:00:29,505
For decades, that search focused on Mars,
on dry riverbeds and ancient lakes.

6
00:00:29,585 --> 00:00:34,717
But some of the most promising places
are far colder and farther from the Sun.

7
00:00:34,797 --> 00:00:40,367
They are small, frozen moons circling
the giant planets Jupiter and Saturn.

8
00:00:40,447 --> 00:00:44,301
Their surfaces are locked
in ice as hard as rock.

9
00:00:44,381 --> 00:00:47,889
Yet beneath that ice, there may be oceans.

10
00:00:50,239 --> 00:00:54,645
In January sixteen ten, Galileo
Galilei pointed one of the first

11
00:00:54,725 --> 00:01:00,142
telescopes at Jupiter and noticed
four little points of light beside it.

12
00:01:00,222 --> 00:01:03,290
They were moons,
and one of them was Europa.

13
00:01:03,370 --> 00:01:06,330
Europa is a little
smaller than our own Moon,

14
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:10,562
and it circles Jupiter once
every three and a half days.

15
00:01:10,642 --> 00:01:15,334
Its surface is a shell of water ice,
bright and strangely smooth.

16
00:01:15,414 --> 00:01:19,648
There are very few craters,
which tells us the surface is young,

17
00:01:19,728 --> 00:01:23,401
perhaps only a few tens
of millions of years old.

18
00:01:23,481 --> 00:01:25,573
Something keeps renewing it.

19
00:01:25,723 --> 00:01:29,511
Long cracks and ridges cross
the ice in every direction,

20
00:01:29,591 --> 00:01:33,760
as if the shell were broken
and mended again and again.

21
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,893
And in places, jumbled
blocks of ice look like

22
00:01:36,973 --> 00:01:40,681
rafts that drifted apart
and froze back together.

23
00:01:40,761 --> 00:01:45,041
Along many of those cracks,
the ice is stained a reddish brown.

24
00:01:45,121 --> 00:01:48,556
Scientists suspect the color
comes from salts and other

25
00:01:48,636 --> 00:01:52,266
chemicals that have worked
their way up from below.

26
00:01:52,346 --> 00:01:56,444
Europa also orbits inside
Jupiter's radiation belts,

27
00:01:56,524 --> 00:02:02,266
so its surface is constantly
bombarded by high-energy particles.

28
00:02:04,116 --> 00:02:07,235
What could warm a world
so far from the Sun?

29
00:02:07,435 --> 00:02:09,709
The answer is gravity.

30
00:02:09,859 --> 00:02:14,389
Europa does not travel in a
perfect circle around Jupiter.

31
00:02:14,469 --> 00:02:18,817
The neighboring moons Io and Ganymede
tug on it in a steady rhythm,

32
00:02:18,897 --> 00:02:21,712
keeping its orbit slightly stretched.

33
00:02:21,792 --> 00:02:25,318
So Europa sometimes swings
closer to giant Jupiter,

34
00:02:25,398 --> 00:02:28,313
and sometimes a little farther away.

35
00:02:28,393 --> 00:02:32,393
Each time, Jupiter's gravity
squeezes and stretches the moon,

36
00:02:32,473 --> 00:02:36,989
the way you warm a piece of clay
by kneading it in your hands.

37
00:02:37,069 --> 00:02:40,754
This flexing creates friction,
and friction makes heat.

38
00:02:40,834 --> 00:02:44,518
Deep inside Europa,
that heat can keep water liquid,

39
00:02:44,598 --> 00:02:49,830
even though the surface is far
colder than any freezer on Earth.

40
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:53,371
In the nineteen nineties,

41
00:02:53,451 --> 00:02:57,999
NASA's Galileo spacecraft flew
past Europa again and again.

42
00:02:58,079 --> 00:03:01,387
Its instruments noticed
something remarkable.

43
00:03:01,537 --> 00:03:06,143
Jupiter's powerful magnetic field
seemed to be disturbed near the moon,

44
00:03:06,223 --> 00:03:10,553
as if something beneath the ice
were conducting electricity.

45
00:03:10,633 --> 00:03:13,337
Salty water does exactly that.

46
00:03:13,487 --> 00:03:16,823
Scientists concluded that
Europa hides a global

47
00:03:16,903 --> 00:03:20,397
ocean of salty water
beneath its icy shell.

48
00:03:20,477 --> 00:03:25,032
That ocean may be sixty to one
hundred and fifty kilometers deep,

49
00:03:25,112 --> 00:03:30,331
holding about twice as much water
as all of Earth's oceans combined.

50
00:03:30,411 --> 00:03:34,940
Above it, the ice shell may be only
ten to thirty kilometers thick.

51
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:38,428
In twenty twenty three,
NASA's James Webb Space

52
00:03:38,508 --> 00:03:42,663
Telescope found carbon
dioxide on Europa's surface.

53
00:03:42,743 --> 00:03:47,462
It was concentrated in a region where
the ice looks recently broken up,

54
00:03:47,542 --> 00:03:51,833
a hint that the carbon may
have come from the ocean below.

55
00:03:51,913 --> 00:03:54,789
The Hubble Space
Telescope has even spotted

56
00:03:54,869 --> 00:03:57,539
hints of water vapor rising from Europa,

57
00:03:57,619 --> 00:04:01,795
though astronomers are still
debating what it means.

58
00:04:04,145 --> 00:04:07,184
Picture Europa cut in half like an apple.

59
00:04:07,264 --> 00:04:11,590
On the outside, a shell of ice,
cold and hard as steel.

60
00:04:11,670 --> 00:04:14,570
Beneath it, a deep, dark, salty ocean.

61
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:20,094
Beneath the ocean, a floor of rock, and at
the center of the moon, a core of metal.

62
00:04:20,174 --> 00:04:23,320
That rocky seafloor matters enormously.

63
00:04:23,470 --> 00:04:26,752
Where hot water meets rock,
minerals dissolve,

64
00:04:26,832 --> 00:04:30,783
chemical reactions begin,
and energy is released.

65
00:04:30,863 --> 00:04:34,117
At the top, the radiation
raining on the surface

66
00:04:34,197 --> 00:04:37,414
creates oxygen-rich chemicals in the ice.

67
00:04:37,494 --> 00:04:41,221
If cracks and slow motions
carry them down into the ocean,

68
00:04:41,301 --> 00:04:46,923
they could mix with the chemistry below,
a little like a battery with two poles.

69
00:04:47,003 --> 00:04:50,781
Nobody knows yet whether
this really happens.

70
00:04:52,631 --> 00:04:57,861
Now travel more than six hundred million
kilometers farther out, to Saturn.

71
00:04:57,941 --> 00:05:03,156
Among its many moons is a tiny,
dazzling world called Enceladus.

72
00:05:03,236 --> 00:05:06,448
It is only about five
hundred kilometers across,

73
00:05:06,528 --> 00:05:10,944
small enough to fit inside the
borders of a single country.

74
00:05:11,024 --> 00:05:15,193
Yet it is one of the brightest
objects in the entire Solar System,

75
00:05:15,273 --> 00:05:18,990
reflecting almost all the
sunlight that falls on it.

76
00:05:19,070 --> 00:05:23,867
Fresh, clean ice covers it,
like snow that fell only yesterday.

77
00:05:23,947 --> 00:05:28,212
Scientists once guessed it was
simply a frozen, lifeless ball.

78
00:05:28,292 --> 00:05:33,873
Then a spacecraft named Cassini arrived
at Saturn, and everything changed.

79
00:05:35,723 --> 00:05:38,555
In two thousand five,
Cassini flew close to

80
00:05:38,635 --> 00:05:41,850
Enceladus and found something astonishing.

81
00:05:41,930 --> 00:05:45,439
Near the moon's south pole,
long cracks nicknamed tiger

82
00:05:45,519 --> 00:05:49,349
stripes were glowing warmer
than the ice around them.

83
00:05:49,429 --> 00:05:54,715
And from those cracks, jets of ice and
vapor were blasting out into space.

84
00:05:54,795 --> 00:05:59,799
Dozens of geysers were shooting
material hundreds of kilometers high.

85
00:05:59,879 --> 00:06:04,366
This spray is so plentiful that
it feeds one of Saturn's rings,

86
00:06:04,446 --> 00:06:08,729
a faint, wide band of ice
particles called the E ring.

87
00:06:08,809 --> 00:06:12,846
The water was coming from
somewhere inside Enceladus.

88
00:06:12,926 --> 00:06:16,290
Careful measurements of the
moon's tiny wobble later

89
00:06:16,370 --> 00:06:20,183
showed that its icy shell
is not frozen to the core.

90
00:06:20,263 --> 00:06:22,818
A global ocean lies beneath it.

91
00:06:22,968 --> 00:06:27,192
The heat pouring out of that
region is far greater than a small,

92
00:06:27,272 --> 00:06:29,996
icy moon should be able to produce.

93
00:06:30,076 --> 00:06:34,674
Enceladus is helped by a gravitational
dance with its neighbor Dione,

94
00:06:34,754 --> 00:06:37,361
which keeps flexing its interior.

95
00:06:37,441 --> 00:06:41,868
Scientists are still working out
exactly how it stays so active.

96
00:06:43,718 --> 00:06:47,398
Cassini did something no
spacecraft had done before.

97
00:06:47,478 --> 00:06:52,487
It flew straight through the geysers and
tasted the spray with its instruments.

98
00:06:52,567 --> 00:06:57,869
The plume contained water, of course,
along with salts, just like seawater.

99
00:06:57,949 --> 00:07:00,951
It also held carbon-based
organic molecules,

100
00:07:01,031 --> 00:07:05,183
the building blocks of
chemistry that life depends on.

101
00:07:05,263 --> 00:07:10,940
Tiny grains of silica revealed that hot
water is touching rock on the ocean floor,

102
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:15,392
at temperatures of around
ninety degrees Celsius or more.

103
00:07:15,472 --> 00:07:22,126
Cassini also detected molecular hydrogen,
a gas that microbes can use as food.

104
00:07:22,206 --> 00:07:26,584
And years later, scientists
studying its data found phosphates,

105
00:07:26,664 --> 00:07:32,272
a form of phosphorus, another essential
ingredient for life as we know it.

106
00:07:32,352 --> 00:07:35,813
A hidden ocean, warm rock,
and the right chemistry,

107
00:07:35,893 --> 00:07:39,179
all on a moon the size of a small country.

108
00:07:39,259 --> 00:07:43,544
In September twenty seventeen,
after thirteen years at Saturn,

109
00:07:43,624 --> 00:07:48,711
Cassini ended its mission by plunging
deliberately into the planet.

110
00:07:48,791 --> 00:07:53,297
Engineers did this on purpose,
so that the spacecraft could never

111
00:07:53,377 --> 00:07:58,564
crash into Enceladus and contaminate
it with microbes from Earth.

112
00:08:00,414 --> 00:08:02,877
Could anything live in a dark ocean,

113
00:08:02,957 --> 00:08:07,018
under kilometers of ice,
with no sunlight at all?

114
00:08:07,218 --> 00:08:09,649
On Earth, the answer is yes.

115
00:08:09,799 --> 00:08:13,277
In nineteen seventy seven,
scientists exploring the

116
00:08:13,357 --> 00:08:17,423
deep sea found hot springs
called hydrothermal vents.

117
00:08:17,503 --> 00:08:23,334
Around them, in total darkness, thrived
crowds of worms, shrimp, and bacteria.

118
00:08:23,414 --> 00:08:25,676
They do not live on sunlight.

119
00:08:25,826 --> 00:08:30,228
They live on chemical energy flowing
out of the rock beneath them.

120
00:08:30,308 --> 00:08:34,712
That discovery changed how we
think about where life can exist.

121
00:08:34,792 --> 00:08:39,293
The oceans of Europa and Enceladus
may have similar ingredients:

122
00:08:39,373 --> 00:08:42,177
liquid water, heat, and chemistry.

123
00:08:42,257 --> 00:08:45,499
But we must be honest. We
have not found life there,

124
00:08:45,579 --> 00:08:48,224
and we do not know if it exists.

125
00:08:50,074 --> 00:08:52,783
To find out more, we are going back.

126
00:08:52,863 --> 00:08:57,365
In October twenty twenty four,
NASA launched Europa Clipper,

127
00:08:57,445 --> 00:09:01,176
a spacecraft built to
study Europa up close.

128
00:09:01,256 --> 00:09:04,595
It is expected to reach
Jupiter in twenty thirty.

129
00:09:04,675 --> 00:09:08,847
From there it will make about
fifty close flybys of Europa.

130
00:09:08,927 --> 00:09:12,041
It carries cameras,
an ice-penetrating radar,

131
00:09:12,121 --> 00:09:16,749
and instruments to taste the
thin atmosphere and any plumes.

132
00:09:16,829 --> 00:09:20,550
Jupiter's radiation is so
fierce that Clipper's most

133
00:09:20,630 --> 00:09:25,094
sensitive electronics sit
inside a thick vault of metal.

134
00:09:25,174 --> 00:09:27,956
Each flyby will pass close to the surface,

135
00:09:28,036 --> 00:09:33,313
then swing back out of the worst of
the radiation to send its data home.

136
00:09:33,393 --> 00:09:37,389
Europe's own mission,
the European Space Agency's JUICE,

137
00:09:37,469 --> 00:09:41,042
is already on its way
to the Jupiter system.

138
00:09:41,122 --> 00:09:44,601
It will study Jupiter's icy
moons, including Europa,

139
00:09:44,681 --> 00:09:47,652
and the largest of them all, Ganymede.

140
00:09:47,732 --> 00:09:51,809
None of these missions is
designed to find life directly.

141
00:09:51,889 --> 00:09:56,495
Their job is to find out whether
these oceans could be habitable.

142
00:09:58,345 --> 00:10:02,654
Europa and Enceladus are
not the only ocean worlds.

143
00:10:02,734 --> 00:10:07,027
Evidence suggests Ganymede and
Callisto may hide oceans too,

144
00:10:07,107 --> 00:10:12,361
and Saturn's giant moon Titan may
have one beneath its orange haze.

145
00:10:12,441 --> 00:10:18,349
If so, the Solar System may hold far
more liquid water than anyone imagined.

146
00:10:18,429 --> 00:10:22,107
Most of it would be hidden,
in the dark, under the ice.

147
00:10:22,187 --> 00:10:27,468
The question of whether we are alone
is one of the oldest questions we ask.

148
00:10:27,548 --> 00:10:32,117
For the first time in history,
we have the tools to look for the answer,

149
00:10:32,197 --> 00:10:35,602
in places we once thought
were frozen and dead.

150
00:10:35,682 --> 00:10:39,005
Each answer we find will
lead to new questions.

151
00:10:39,085 --> 00:10:43,985
Even if these oceans turn out to be
empty, they would teach us how rare,

152
00:10:44,065 --> 00:10:47,894
or how common, the conditions
for life really are.

153
00:10:47,974 --> 00:10:52,468
Somewhere out there, beneath the
ice, an ocean may be waiting.
